In Java, the `==` operator compares the references of two objects, not their values. This means that it checks if both operands refer to the same object in memory. For strings, `==` operator only returns true if both operands are references to the same String object, not if they have the same value. 

On the other hand, the `.equals()` method is a standard method provided by the `String` class that compares the values of two strings, regardless of the object references. It is the recommended way to compare strings in Java.

In summary, you should use the `.equals()` method instead of the `==` operator to compare strings in Java. Use `==` only when you want to check if two variables refer to the same object (e.g., when working with custom objects or when comparing primitive data types).

Here's an example:

```java
String str1 = "Hello, world!";
String str2 = "Hello, world!";

// Using == operator
if (str1 == str2) {
    System.out.println("Both strings refer to the same object");
} else {
    System.out.println("Both strings do not refer to the same object");
}

// Using .equals() method
if (str1.equals(str2)) {
    System.out.println("Both strings have the same value");
} else {
    System.out.println("Both strings do not have the same value");
}
```

